IPI callback and local writer lifetime
======================================

Run ipi_lifetime_test.sh, instance_lookup_test.sh, instance_release_test.sh
and ipi_writer_test.sh with bash. CC and CFLAGS select the host compiler and
sanitizers. Each runner extracts the production functions, not another
implementation. Kernel RCU, scheduler, IDR, physical IPI and hardware boundaries
are substituted; these tests do not qualify kernel IRQ/RCU behavior.

The callback test holds an entered callback while another pthread unregisters
it. The baseline freed its handler before that callback returned. The fixed
path unlinks under the writer lock, then waits outside that lock for an RCU
grace period covering lookup and callback invocation. The test requires an
active protected reader, not merely entry into the grace-period shim. Caller
context remains live until unregister returns. Unregister is process-context
only, must not run inside a callback, and cannot hold callback-owned locks.

Lookup now pins the instance in an RCU read-side section with
kref_get_unless_zero(), without the sleeping registry mutex in a hard-IPI
sender. IDR writers reserve an empty slot under the registry mutex and publish
only after the instance ID is initialized. No allocator runs under a raw
membership lock. Normal destruction returns resources synchronously, so later
fragments of the same overlay can reuse them. The last reference defers record
and persistent boot-page reclamation to process context after an RCU grace
period. Early-publication failure also drops its reference instead of freeing
an object another lookup can have pinned.

Local producers hold the per-instance raw ring lock through payload publication,
but release it before ringing a doorbell or logging. Reset closes publication
under that lock, clears the persistent ring with IRQs enabled, then republishes
it and a cached physical target. Atomic senders do not walk a mutable CPU set.
The fixture exercises send, closed admission and reset/republication ordering.
Message sends now build the header and payload directly in the reserved slot,
under the same producer lock, without slab allocation or a full-size stack
buffer. Publication follows both copies. Raw IPI callers keep their existing
interface. The wire size remains sizeof(struct mk_message) plus payload length,
with payload at its original flexible-array offset and zero tail padding.
NULL payloads retain the previous zero-fill behavior. A held-instance send does
not acquire or drop the caller's reference; an ID send pins through publication.
The fixture checks 16 size/NULL-payload/route combinations, full-size messages,
overflow rejection, queue/deactivation errors and bytes at release publication.
The target cache is refreshed at publication/reset and local CPU ownership
changes, except for the firmware-described parent doorbell target. Runtime
boot-hart reassignment remains a hardware qualification gate.

The host-owned ring allocation now outlives the Image grant on this upstream
base. Host instance references therefore pin its storage. These local locks
do not fence foreign kernels writing the same physical ring: remote producer
retirement, requester restart and the shared receive-ring reset remain protocol
limitations, not solved by resetting indices or confirming receiver CPUs.
No #7 protocol dependency, new message layout or mixed-peer claim is introduced.
mktty IRQ locking and higher-level messaging registration admission are separate
baseline gates; callback retirement alone does not qualify them.
